---
import QuestionLayout from '@layouts/QuestionLayout.astro';

const relatedLinks = [];
const _body = "<p>Traverse produces a typed error, with a stable <code>code</code>, a message, and structured details, at whichever point in the execution pipeline the request stops. The most common categories are input validation before execution, selection failures, execution itself, and output validation after.</p>\n\n      <h2>Input and selection failures</h2>\n      <p>Before the WASM binary runs, the caller's input is checked against the contract's input schema; a mismatch returns <code>request_invalid</code>. Separately, candidate selection can fail to find a runnable capability at all — that returns <code>capability_not_found</code> (nothing matched) or <code>capability_not_runnable</code> (something matched but couldn't run in this placement). These are hard stops — no partial execution happens. (A contract's declared <code>preconditions</code> are documentation for reviewers today, not something the runtime evaluates as part of this check.)</p>\n\n      <h2>Execution errors</h2>\n      <p>If the WASM binary itself traps or returns an error, the runtime captures it as <code>execution_failed</code>. The state machine moves to the <code>error</code> terminal state. The trace artifact records the input, the execution attempt, and the failure reason. You do not get a partial output.</p>\n\n      <h2>Output validation failures</h2>\n      <p>After the WASM binary returns a result, the output is checked against the contract's output schema. If it does not satisfy that schema, the result is discarded and <code>output_validation_failed</code> is returned to the caller instead. This is a safeguard against implementations that return a value the schema doesn't allow. (A contract's declared <code>postconditions</code> are, likewise, documentation today rather than a separate runtime-evaluated check.)</p>\n\n      <h2>What errors carry</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>A stable error <code>code</code> (e.g. <code>request_invalid</code>, <code>capability_not_found</code>, <code>execution_failed</code>, <code>output_validation_failed</code>)</li>\n        <li>A human-readable message and structured details about what triggered it</li>\n        <li>The state machine phase where the failure occurred</li>\n        <li>The trace artifact up to the point of failure</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Error handling for AI agents</h2>\n      <p>When a capability fails via the MCP server, the error goes back to the agent as a structured response. The agent can read the error code and details and adjust its inputs rather than retrying blindly. See <a href=\"/questions/how-does-traverse-prevent-ai-agent-errors.html\">how Traverse prevents AI agent errors</a> for more.</p>\n\n      <p>See also: <a href=\"/questions/what-happens-when-a-capability-fails.html\">what happens when a capability fails</a>.</p>";
const _jsonLd = "{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"FAQPage\",\"mainEntity\":[{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"How does Traverse handle errors?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Traverse returns a typed error with a stable code at whichever stage a request stops: request_invalid or capability_not_found/capability_not_runnable before execution, execution_failed during WASM execution, and output_validation_failed if the result doesn't satisfy the output schema. Each error carries its code, a message, structured details, and the trace artifact up to the point of failure.\"}}]}";
---
<QuestionLayout
  title={"How does Traverse handle errors?"}
  description={"Traverse returns typed errors (request_invalid, capability_not_found, execution_failed, output_validation_failed, and others) with a stable code, message, and structured details for debugging."}
  canonical={"https://traverse-framework.com/questions/how-does-traverse-handle-errors.html"}
  category={"Technical Architecture"}
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={_jsonLd}
>
  <Fragment set:html={_body} />
</QuestionLayout>
